<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>How Comparison Works &mdash; globus-sdk Migratability Report</title>
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
{% include "_styles.html.jinja" %}
</head>
<body>
  {% include "_nav.html.jinja" %}
  <h1>How the comparison works</h1>
  <div class="meta">
    Generated {{ report.generated_at }} &middot; globus-sdk version <code>{{ report.sdk_version }}</code>.
    Every example on this page is pulled live from this run's actual results &mdash;
    not hand-written &mdash; so it reflects the matcher's real behavior against the
    current SDK and specs.
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <h2>1. Finding each SDK method's route</h2>
    <p>
      Every <code>globus-sdk</code> client method documents the HTTP route it calls in
      its docstring. Most do it as an inline code span:
    </p>
    <pre><code>``GET /v2/groups/my_groups``</code></pre>
    <p>
      A few services (flows, compute) instead use a Sphinx <code>.. extdoclink::</code>
      directive whose <code>:ref:</code> encodes the OpenAPI path or <code>operationId</code>
      instead, e.g. <code>Flows/paths/~1flows/post</code> (a JSON-Pointer-escaped path plus
      method) or <code>Endpoints/operation/register_endpoint_v2_endpoints_post</code>
      (a tag plus operationId). The tool parses both forms; see
      <code>sdk_introspect.py</code>'s <code>_extract_route</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <h2>2. Matching SDK route to OpenAPI operation</h2>
    <p>
      The extracted route is normalized &mdash; <code>&lt;group_id&gt;</code> and
      <code>{group_id}</code> path parameters both become a wildcard segment &mdash;
      and matched against the OpenAPI spec's operations by
      <strong>(HTTP method, path template)</strong>, or directly by
      <strong>operationId</strong> when a path couldn't be recovered. Here is a real
      match from this run:
    </p>
    {% if examples.clean_match %}
    <div class="example">
      <h3>Match: {{ examples.clean_match.service }} &mdash; {{ examples.clean_match.method.label }}</h3>
      <div class="example-cols">
        <div>
          <h4>globus-sdk docstring</h4>
          <pre><code>{{ sdk_blob(examples.clean_match.method.sdk_method) }}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4>OpenAPI operation</h4>
          <pre><code>{{ openapi_blob(examples.clean_match.method.openapi_op) }}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="verdict">
        {% for line in verdict_lines(examples.clean_match.method) %}
        <div>&#10003; {{ line }}</div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
    {% else %}
    <p class="missing-example-note">No clean, aberration-free match was found in this run.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <h2>3. Scoring parameter &amp; response fidelity</h2>
    <p>
      For a matched pair, the OpenAPI operation's query and request-body field names are
      checked against the SDK method: a field counts as covered if it's an explicit
      keyword argument, <em>or</em> its name appears as a literal string in the method's
      source (the common pattern of building a <code>query_params={...}</code> or
      <code>data={...}</code> dict). This is a heuristic, not a type-level diff &mdash;
      it is meant to surface likely drift for a human to double-check.
    </p>
    <p>
      The response shape is checked too: if the OpenAPI response schema's type is
      <code>array</code>, the SDK method is expected to return an array-like wrapper
      (<code>ArrayResponse</code>, <code>IterableResponse</code>, ...); a mismatch is
      flagged.
    </p>
    <p>The overall fidelity score is 50% baseline (for having a match at all) + up to 35%
      for parameter coverage + up to 15% for response-shape agreement.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <h2>4. Aberrations this tool looks for</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Kind</th><th>Severity</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>missing_required_field</code></td><td><span class="aberr error">error</span></td>
          <td>OpenAPI marks a field required, but it's not referenced anywhere in the matched SDK method.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>missing_optional_field</code></td><td><span class="aberr warning">warning</span></td>
          <td>OpenAPI defines an optional field the SDK method doesn't appear to reference.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sdk_only_parameter</code></td><td><span class="aberr info">info</span></td>
          <td>The SDK method exposes a keyword argument with no obviously corresponding OpenAPI field.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>response_shape_mismatch</code></td><td><span class="aberr warning">warning</span></td>
          <td>OpenAPI says the response is (or isn't) an array, but the SDK's response wrapper disagrees.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>no_matching_operation</code></td><td><span class="aberr warning">warning</span></td>
          <td>The SDK method declares a route that doesn't exist in the current OpenAPI spec (removed, moved, or the spec changed).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>missing_from_sdk</code></td><td><span class="aberr error">error</span></td>
          <td>The OpenAPI spec defines an operation with no corresponding SDK method at all.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>undocumented_route</code></td><td><span class="aberr info">info</span></td>
          <td>The SDK method's docstring has no parseable route or operationId reference, so it couldn't be matched either way.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A separate <code>no_spec</code> status (not an aberration) applies to whole services with no
      fetchable OpenAPI spec at all &mdash; Transfer, Auth, and GCS, as of writing. Their SDK methods
      are still listed in the report, at 0% coverage, for completeness; nothing about them is
      actually being compared.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <h2>5. Worked examples of things that don't match</h2>

    {% macro one_sided_example(ex, title, side) %}
      {% if ex %}
      <div class="example">
        <h3>{{ title }}: {{ ex.service }} &mdash; {{ ex.method.label }}</h3>
        <div class="example-cols">
          <div>
            <h4>globus-sdk docstring</h4>
            <pre><code>{{ sdk_blob(ex.method.sdk_method) }}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4>OpenAPI operation</h4>
            <pre><code>{{ openapi_blob(ex.method.openapi_op) }}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="verdict">
          {% for line in verdict_lines(ex.method) %}
          <div>&#10007; {{ line }}</div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>
      {% else %}
      <p class="missing-example-note">No current example of this kind ({{ title|lower }}) was found in this run.</p>
      {% endif %}
    {% endmacro %}

    {{ one_sided_example(examples.missing_from_sdk, "Missing from SDK", "spec") }}
    {{ one_sided_example(examples.no_matching_operation, "SDK route not in spec", "sdk") }}
    {{ one_sided_example(examples.missing_required_field, "Missing required field", "both") }}
    {{ one_sided_example(examples.sdk_only_param, "SDK-only parameter", "both") }}
    {{ one_sided_example(examples.response_shape_mismatch, "Response shape mismatch", "both") }}
  </div>

  <footer class="meta">
    Built by <a href="https://github.com/{{ repo }}">{{ repo }}</a>. See
    <code>src/globus_migratability/compare.py</code> and
    <code>src/globus_migratability/sdk_introspect.py</code> for the full matching
    implementation this page describes.
  </footer>
</body>
</html>
